<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · Celebration · 1556 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Ríu Ríu Chíu</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="es">Ríu, ríu, chíu</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Spanish Christmas song printed in 1556. Its &quot;ríu ríu chíu&quot; may be a bird's call, and it sings of God keeping the wolf from the lamb.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous (attributed by some sources to Mateo Flecha the Elder)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>anonymous (attributed by some sources to Mateo Flecha the Elder)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Spanish</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Spain (printed in Venice)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>printed 1556</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · Celebration</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (anonymous, or by Mateo Flecha the Elder (d. 1553); printed in the Cancionero de Upsala (Venice, 1556))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riu-riu-chiu">Ríu Ríu Chíu</a>&quot;, sometimes shortened to &quot;Ríu Ríu&quot; or &quot;Ríu Chíu&quot;, is a Spanish villancico, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> song now sung as a Christmas carol and tied to the Feast of the Immaculate Conception during Advent. Some sources attribute it to Mateo Flecha the Elder, who died in 1553; others call it anonymous. It closely resembles another villancico, &quot;Falalanlera&quot;, by the Aragonese composer Bartomeu Càrceres. It survives in a single source, the Cancionero de Upsala, printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1556, where it is the fortieth song; the one known copy is kept in the library of the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppsala">Uppsala</a>. The musicologist Daniel R. Melamed called it &quot;redoubtable&quot; and a contender for the best-known piece of Renaissance music.</p><p>The apparently nonsense syllables of its refrain are often taken for the song of a nightingale, though the context and the etymology fit the call of a kingfisher; &quot;riu&quot; is also the Catalan word for river, the sense in which the Monkees later sang it. The song's theme is the birth of Christ. It tells of God guarding the Lamb from the wolf, and of the Virgin defended from the raging wolf and free of original sin; it speaks of the Incarnation, of the prophecies fulfilled, and of a thousand angels singing to the shepherds of glory in heaven and peace on earth.</p><p>Early-music ensembles took it up in the 20th century, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> Camerata, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> Camerata, the Waverly Consort (1977), the King's Singers (1989) and Chanticleer (1990). The Kingston Trio recorded it as &quot;Guardo el Lobo&quot; in 1961, credited to the musicologist Erich Schwandt, and Chip Douglas sang it with the Modern Folk Quartet in 1964 before, as the Monkees' producer, he brought it to the group, who performed it on their Christmas television episode in 1967. It was sung in Spanish at King's College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, in the broadcast Nine Lessons and Carols of Christmas Eve 1992.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A Christmas villancico about how God kept the wolf from his Lamb: the Virgin, whom the wolf sought to bite, was preserved from original sin, and the child now born is the great King, God made flesh, whose birth the angels announce with peace to the world. Its bird-call refrain makes it bright and dancing.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Spanish</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Spanish</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Riu, riu, chiu, la guarda ribera,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Riu, riu, chiu, the riverbank guard [?],</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Dios guardó el lobo a nuestra cordera.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>God kept the wolf from our ewe-lamb.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">El lobo rabioso la quiso morder,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The raging wolf wanted to bite her,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Mas Dios poderoso la supo defender,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>But mighty God knew how to defend her,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Quízole hazer que no pudiesse pecar,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He chose to make her so that she could not sin,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Ni aún original esta virgen no tuviera.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Nor would this virgin have even original sin.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Riu, riu, chiu, la guarda ribera,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Riu, riu, chiu, the riverbank guard [?],</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Dios guardó el lobo a nuestra cordera.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>God kept the wolf from our ewe-lamb.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Este qu'es nasçido es el gran monarcha,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>This one who is born is the great monarch,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Christo patriarcha de carne vestido.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Christ the patriarch clothed in flesh.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Anos [Ha nos] redimido con se hazer chiquito,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He has redeemed us by making himself little,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Aunque era infinito, finito se hiziera.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Though he was infinite, he made himself finite.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Riu, riu, chiu, la guarda ribera,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Riu, riu, chiu, the riverbank guard [?],</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Dios guardó el lobo a nuestra cordera.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>God kept the wolf from our ewe-lamb.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Éste viene a dar a los muertos vida,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>This one comes to give life to the dead,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Y viene a reparar de todos la cayda;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And comes to repair the fall of all;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Es la luz del dia aqueste moçuelo,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>This little lad is the light of day,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Este es el cordero que San Juan dixera.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>This is the lamb that Saint John spoke of.</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 44 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Riu, riu, chiu, la guarda ribera,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Riu, riu, chiu, the riverbank guard [?],</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Dios guardó el lobo a nuestra cordera.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>God kept the wolf from our ewe-lamb.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Muchas profecías lo an profetizado,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Many prophecies have foretold him,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Y aún en nuestros días, lo hemos alcançado,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And even in our days we have lived to see it,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">A Dios humanado vemos en el suelo,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>God made man we see on the earth,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Y al hombre en el cielo porque'l lo quisiera.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And man in heaven, because he willed it.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Riu, riu, chiu, la guarda ribera,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Riu, riu, chiu, the riverbank guard [?],</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Dios guardó el lobo a nuestra cordera.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>God kept the wolf from our ewe-lamb.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Mira bien que os cuadre que ansina lo oyera,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Mark well, and let it suit you, that so I heard it [?],</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Que Dios no pudiera hazerla más que madre;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>That God could not have made her more than mother;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">El qu'era su Padre, oy d'ella nasçió,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He who was her Father was born of her today,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Y el que la crió, su Hijo se dixera.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And he who created her was called her Son.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Riu, riu, chiu, la guarda ribera,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Riu, riu, chiu, the riverbank guard [?],</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Dios guardó el lobo a nuestra cordera.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>God kept the wolf from our ewe-lamb.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Yo vi mil garçones que andavan cantando.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I saw a thousand lads who went about singing.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Por aquí volando haziendo mil sones,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Flying about here, making a thousand sounds,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Diziendo a gascones, Gloria sea en el cielo,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Saying to Gascons [?]: Glory be in heaven,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Y paz en el suelo pues Jesús nasçiera.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And peace on earth, for Jesus was born.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Riu, riu, chiu, la guarda ribera,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Riu, riu, chiu, the riverbank guard [?],</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Dios guardó el lobo a nuestra cordera.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>God kept the wolf from our ewe-lamb.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Pues que ya tenemos lo que desseamos,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Since now we have what we desired,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Todos juntos vamos presentes llevemos;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let us all go together, let us take gifts;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Todos le daremos nuestra voluntad,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We will all give him our will,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Pues a se igualar con nosotros viniera</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Since he came to make himself equal with us</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Riu, riu, chiu, la guarda ribera,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Riu, riu, chiu, the riverbank guard [?],</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Dios guardó el lobo a nuestra cordera.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>God kept the wolf from our ewe-lamb.</span></p></div></details></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Anonymous, attributed to Mateo Flecha the Elder; Cancionero de Upsala (Venice, 1556). Text from <a href="https://es.wikisource.org/wiki/Riu%2C_riu%2C_chiu" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (13)</summary><ul><li>Refrain, line 1: 'Riu, riu, chiu' imitates a bird's call. 'La guarda ribera' is obscure: it may mean 'the guard of the riverbank' or be read as an imperative, 'guard the riverbank'.</li><li>Refrain, line 2: literally 'God guarded the wolf from our ewe-lamb'; cordera, a female lamb, stands for the Virgin Mary.</li><li>Line 2.3: quízole hazer, 'he willed to make her'.</li><li>Line 2.4: 'original' stands for original sin.</li><li>Line 4.3: the edition prints 'Anos [Ha nos]', 'has us', i.e. 'he has redeemed us'.</li><li>Line 6.2: cayda (caída), the Fall.</li><li>Line 6.3: moçuelo (mozuelo), a lad, a young boy.</li><li>Line 8.2: lo hemos alcançado, 'we have reached it', i.e. lived to see it.</li><li>Line 10.1: the sense of the line is unclear; ansina is an old rustic form of así, 'thus', and oyera may be 'I heard' or 'he heard'.</li><li>Line 12.1: garçones, young men or lads; here the angels.</li><li>Line 12.3: a gascones, literally 'to Gascons'; the sense of the word here is uncertain.</li><li>Line 14.3: voluntad, 'will', also 'goodwill' or 'love'.</li><li>Line 14.4: the edition has no closing punctuation.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
